{"task_id": "HumanEval/35", "solution": "def max_element(l: list):\n    \"\"\"Return maximum element in the list.\n    >>> max_element([1, 2, 3])\n    3\n    >>> max_element([5, 3, -5, 2, -3, 3, 9, 0, 123, 1, -10])\n    123\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty.\")\n\n    max_val = l[0]\n    for element in l[1:]:\n        if element > max_val:\n            max_val = element\n    return max_val"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/36", "solution": "```python\ndef fizz_buzz(n: int):\n    \"\"\"Return the number of times the digit 7 appears in integers less than n which are divisible by 11 or 13.\n    >>> fizz_buzz(50)\n    0\n    >>> fizz_buzz(78)\n    2\n    >>> fizz_buzz(79)\n    3\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sevens = 0\n    # Iterate through numbers from 1 up to n-1.\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        # Check if the number is divisible by 11 or 13.\n        if (i % 11 == 0) or (i % 13 == 0):\n            # Convert the number to a string to count occurrences of the digit '7'.\n            total_sevens += str(i).count('7')\n    return total_sevens\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_third(l: list):\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n    indices_to_sort = []\n    elements_to_sort = []\n\n    # Collect elements and their original indices that are divisible by three.\n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            indices_to_sort.append(i)\n            elements_to_sort.append(l[i])\n\n    # Sort the collected elements.\n    elements_to_sort.sort()\n\n    # Create a shallow copy of the original list to modify.\n    result_list = list(l)\n\n    # Replace elements in the result list at the identified indices with their sorted counterparts.\n    for i, original_index in enumerate(indices_to_sort):\n        result_list[original_index] = elements_to_sort[i]\n\n    return result_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/37", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_even(l: list):\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the odd indicies, while its values at the even indicies are equal\n    to the values of the even indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_even([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_even([5, 6, 3, 4])\n    [3, 6, 5, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    even_indexed_elements = []\n    for i in range(0, len(l), 2):\n        even_indexed_elements.append(l[i])\n    even_indexed_elements.sort()\n\n    result = l[:]  # Create a shallow copy to preserve odd elements and structure.\n\n    even_idx_ptr = 0\n    for i in range(0, len(l), 2):\n        result[i] = even_indexed_elements[even_idx_ptr]\n        even_idx_ptr += 1\n\n    return result\n```"}
