{"task_id": "HumanEval/69", "solution": "```python\ndef search(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates frequencies, identifies candidates where frequency >= value, and returns the greatest such integer.\n    Returns -1 if no such integer is found.\n    \"\"\"\n    frequency_map = {}\n    for num in lst:\n        frequency_map[num] = frequency_map.get(num, 0) + 1\n\n    candidates = []\n    for num, freq in frequency_map.items():\n        if freq >= num:\n            candidates.append(num)\n\n    if not candidates:\n        return -1\n    return max(candidates)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/65", "solution": "```python\ndef circular_shift(x, shift):\n    \"\"\"Circular shift the digits of the integer x, shift the digits right by shift\n    and return the result as a string.\n    If shift > number of digits, return digits reversed.\n    >>> circular_shift(12, 1)\n    \"21\"\n    >>> circular_shift(12, 2)\n    \"12\"\n    \"\"\"\n    is_negative = x < 0\n    abs_x_str = str(abs(x))\n    num_digits = len(abs_x_str)\n\n    if shift > num_digits:\n        # If shift > number of digits, reverse the digits.\n        result_digits = abs_x_str[::-1]\n    else:\n        # Perform a circular right shift.\n        # Handle cases where num_digits is 0 (for x=0, it's \"0\", so num_digits is 1, not 0).\n        # This branch won't be hit for an empty string input as x is an integer.\n        if num_digits == 0:\n            result_digits = \"\"\n        else:\n            effective_shift = shift % num_digits\n            result_digits = abs_x_str[-effective_shift:] + abs_x_str[:-effective_shift]\n\n    return \"-\" + result_digits if is_negative else result_digits\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/70", "solution": "```python\ndef strange_sort_list(lst):\n    '''\n    Given list of integers, return list in strange order.\n    Strange sorting, is when you start with the minimum value,\n    then maximum of the remaining integers, then minimum and so on.\n\n    Examples:\n    strange_sort_list([1, 2, 3, 4]) == [1, 4, 2, 3]\n    strange_sort_list([5, 5, 5, 5]) == [5, 5, 5, 5]\n    strange_sort_list([]) == []\n    '''\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n\n    # Create a mutable sorted copy of the list to easily get min/max.\n    mutable_sorted_list = sorted(lst)\n    result = []\n\n    while mutable_sorted_list:\n        # Append the current minimum element.\n        result.append(mutable_sorted_list.pop(0))\n        if not mutable_sorted_list:\n            break\n        # Append the current maximum element from the remaining list.\n        result.append(mutable_sorted_list.pop(-1))\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/68", "solution": "```python\ndef pluck(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the smallest even value and its first index in the array.\n    Returns [value, index] or an empty list if no even values or the array is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    smallest_even_value = float('inf')\n    smallest_even_index = -1\n\n    if not arr:\n        return []\n\n    for index, value in enumerate(arr):\n        if value % 2 == 0:  # Check if the value is even\n            if value < smallest_even_value:\n                smallest_even_value = value\n                smallest_even_index = index\n            elif value == smallest_even_value and index < smallest_even_index:\n                smallest_even_index = index\n\n    if smallest_even_index != -1:\n        return [smallest_even_value, smallest_even_index]\n    else:\n        return []\n```"}
